content:
{
  "asset": "Pump P-113 — AquaDyne HF-300, serial HF3-22650. Motor replaced yesterday by the electrical contractor; vibration since restart is 4.8 mm/s at the drive end, housing temperature is 70 C, and no leakage is reported.",
  "priority": "P2",
  "stop_equipment_now": false,
  "condition_assessment": [
    {
      "statement": "Vibration: the applicable criterion is HF-300 vibration velocity in mm/s RMS measured on bearing housings. The reported 4.8 mm/s drive-end reading is above the 4.5 mm/s RMS Zone B/C boundary and below the 7.1 mm/s RMS Zone C/D boundary, placing it in Zone C (4.5–7.1): 'Restricted — plan maintenance.' It is not in Zone D ('Damage likely — stop the pump'), so the reported reading supports planned corrective maintenance rather than an immediate vibration-based stop.",
      "citations": [
        "C06",
        "C07"
      ]
    },
    {
      "statement": "The manual requires vibration measurement on both bearing housings and classification of the highest reading. Only the drive-end reading is reported, so Zone C is the minimum classification established by the report; the non-drive-end reading is still needed to complete the classification.",
      "citations": [
        "C06"
      ]
    },
    {
      "statement": "Temperature: the applicable limits are for bearing-housing surface temperature. At 70 C, the reported housing temperature is exactly at the upper end of 'Normal up to 70 C'; it is below the 80 C alarm and 90 C trip. It therefore does not presently exceed a documented temperature limit, although there is no remaining margin within the normal range.",
      "citations": [
        "C01",
        "C02"
      ]
    },
    {
      "statement": "Leakage: none is reported, so the troubleshooting symptom 'leakage at shaft seal' is not presently observed. The evidence provides no numerical allowable-leakage limit; the absence of leakage alone does not establish the internal condition of the seal or justify preventive seal replacement during this vibration investigation.",
      "citations": [
        "C09"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "probable_causes": [
    {
      "statement": "Most likely — shaft misalignment following motor replacement. Misalignment is a listed probable cause of high vibration, the vibration appeared after the motor work, and the manual specifically requires laser alignment after any work that moves the motor or bearing housing. Alignment measurements are required to confirm this suspected cause.",
      "citations": [
        "C09",
        "C10"
      ]
    },
    {
      "statement": "Bearing wear remains a listed cause of high vibration and must be inspected. The reported 70 C housing temperature does not itself exceed the documented normal, alarm or trip limits, and no bearing wear or noise measurements are supplied.",
      "citations": [
        "C02",
        "C09"
      ]
    },
    {
      "statement": "Impeller imbalance or damage is another documented cause of high vibration; no impeller inspection result is available.",
      "citations": [
        "C09"
      ]
    },
    {
      "statement": "Cavitation is also documented as a possible cause. Check NPSH and the suction conditions rather than assuming cavitation, because no NPSH or operating-condition evidence is supplied.",
      "citations": [
        "C09"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "tasks": [
    {
      "statement": "Before corrective work, confirm the drive-end measurement and measure vibration velocity on both bearing housings, recording the values in mm/s RMS. Classify the highest reading using Figure 3. Manage the present 4.8 mm/s result as Zone C planned maintenance; if a reading is classified in Zone D, stop the pump.",
      "citations": [
        "C06",
        "C07"
      ]
    },
    {
      "statement": "Perform and record laser shaft alignment following the motor replacement. Document as-found and final parallel offset and angular alignment. Applicable tolerances are parallel offset 0.05 mm and angular 0.05 mm per 100 mm.",
      "citations": [
        "C10"
      ]
    },
    {
      "statement": "Inspect the bearings and impeller and check NPSH for the remaining documented high-vibration causes. Record bearing wear or noise, impeller imbalance or damage, and any evidence of cavitation; do not condemn components solely from the present temperature reading.",
      "citations": [
        "C02",
        "C09"
      ]
    },
    {
      "statement": "If either bearing shows wear, noise or high temperature, replace both bearings as a pair. If internal access requires removal of the back pull-out assembly, use lifting equipment for its 68 kg mass. For applicable fasteners actually disturbed during reassembly, Table 5 values for lightly oiled threads are: M24 impeller nut 160 Nm, M12 bearing-housing bolts 85 Nm, and M16 casing bolts 210 Nm.",
      "citations": [
        "C02",
        "C10",
        "C11"
      ]
    },
    {
      "statement": "Do not replace the mechanical seal merely because of the reported vibration. If inspection separately establishes that seal replacement is required, isolate and drain the pump, remove the back pull-out assembly, impeller nut and impeller, withdraw the seal and sleeve, and inspect the sleeve for scoring. Fit a new seal and new casing gasket. For this serial, use pre-set HF3-450B, not HF3-450; no sleeve measurement is needed for seal setting. Tighten its seal gland nuts to 30 Nm, and replace shaft sleeve HF3-455 if scoring exceeds 0.1 mm depth.",
      "citations": [
        "C09",
        "C10",
        "C13"
      ]
    },
    {
      "statement": "After alignment and any inspection-based repairs, repeat vibration measurements on both bearing housings and classify the highest result. Target a highest reading below 4.5 mm/s RMS so it is below the restricted Zone C range before return to long-term operation; continue diagnosis if Zone C persists and stop if Zone D is reached. Also recheck housing surface temperature against normal up to 70 C, alarm at 80 C and trip at 90 C.",
      "citations": [
        "C02",
        "C06",
        "C07"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "parts": [],
  "safety_controls": [
    {
      "statement": "The current reported readings do not require an immediate documented-limit stop. Stop the pump if vibration is classified in Zone D, shown as 7.1–11.0 mm/s RMS and labelled 'Damage likely — stop the pump.'",
      "citations": [
        "C07"
      ]
    },
    {
      "statement": "Before the mechanical-seal/back pull-out disassembly procedure, isolate and drain the pump.",
      "citations": [
        "C10"
      ]
    },
    {
      "statement": "Use lifting equipment if removing the back pull-out assembly; its documented mass is 68 kg.",
      "citations": [
        "C02"
      ]
    },
    {
      "statement": "Maintain the documented Plan 11 seal flush from pump discharge and never run the pump dry.",
      "citations": [
        "C02"
      ]
    },
    {
      "statement": "Apply the bearing-housing surface-temperature limits during testing and operation: normal up to 70 C, alarm at 80 C and trip at 90 C.",
      "citations": [
        "C01",
        "C02"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "labour": [
    {
      "statement": "Base documented replacement-follow-up task: laser alignment — 1.5 hours with 1 technician. Inspection, NPSH checking and verification-testing durations are not included in this standard time.",
      "citations": [
        "C12"
      ]
    },
    {
      "statement": "Conditional only if inspection requires bearing replacement: bearing replacement as a pair — 6.0 hours with 2 technicians.",
      "citations": [
        "C10",
        "C12"
      ]
    },
    {
      "statement": "Conditional only if a separate seal-replacement requirement is established: mechanical seal replacement — 4.0 hours with 2 technicians.",
      "citations": [
        "C10",
        "C12"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "source_conflicts": [
    {
      "statement": "Seal part and gland-torque conflict: manual Table 5 lists M10 seal gland nuts at 25 Nm, while Service Bulletin SB-2026-014 requires 30 Nm with cartridge seal HF3-450B. The bulletin was issued 14 March 2026 and applies to HF-300 pumps serial HF3-22000 and above; P-113 is HF3-22650 and therefore falls within its range. For any seal work on this asset, the bulletin governs: do not fit HF3-450, use pre-set HF3-450B with no sleeve measurement for seal setting, apply 30 Nm gland torque, and replace HF3-455 if sleeve scoring exceeds 0.1 mm. Pumps below HF3-22000 remain on HF3-450 and the manual table. This decision does not change the other Table 5 torques or create a present requirement to replace a non-leaking seal.",
      "citations": [
        "C09",
        "C11",
        "C13"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "open_questions": [
    "What is the non-drive-end bearing-housing vibration reading, and was the reported 4.8 mm/s measured as RMS velocity on the bearing housing? Both-housing measurement and highest-reading classification are required by C06 and Figure 3 in C07.",
    "Did the electrical contractor perform laser alignment after installing the motor? If so, what were the as-found and final parallel and angular values, and what alignment tool record is available? The report contains no alignment measurements for comparison with C10.",
    "Which housing was measured at 70 C, and was the reading taken on the bearing-housing surface as specified in C01?",
    "What are the bearing condition/noise findings, impeller condition, suction conditions and available NPSH? The evidence does not distinguish among these remaining causes listed in C09.",
    "No replacement parts are established for the base alignment and diagnostic scope. If inspection expands the scope, bearing and impeller part numbers and the required new casing-gasket part number are not supplied; standard labour times for inspection, NPSH checking and verification testing are also absent from C12."
  ]
}
